WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:02.600
You're not seeing this pink. Not properly.

00:00:02.600 --> 00:00:04.419
This camera can't capture it,

00:00:04.419 --> 00:00:05.640
YouTube can't store it,

00:00:05.640 --> 00:00:07.759
and your screen can't display it.

00:00:07.759 --> 00:00:10.160
I'm in the studio of artist Stuart Semple,

00:00:10.160 --> 00:00:14.320
and he's made what he calls the Pinkest Pink.

00:00:14.320 --> 00:00:16.849
I've been painting since
I was five years old,

00:00:16.849 --> 00:00:19.050
and I've seen a lot of pink.

00:00:19.050 --> 00:00:21.820
And thousands and thousands of people
have used the pink,

00:00:21.820 --> 00:00:24.680
and nobody's said they've seen a pinker pink.

00:00:24.689 --> 00:00:26.039
This stuff's really pink.

00:00:26.039 --> 00:00:29.599
I've created the pinkest pink,
the greenest green,

00:00:29.599 --> 00:00:32.380
the loveliest blue,
and the yellowest yellow.

00:00:32.380 --> 00:00:35.890
I've also created the world's most glittery
natural glitter

00:00:35.890 --> 00:00:40.129
and the mattest blackest
art material in the world.

00:00:40.129 --> 00:00:44.399
About a year and a half ago, a company called
Surrey Nanosystems

00:00:44.399 --> 00:00:46.980
announced that they'd created Vantablack,

00:00:46.980 --> 00:00:51.170
which was this really awesome
blacker-than-the-blackest-black black

00:00:51.170 --> 00:00:54.720
but they gave the exclusive rights to an artist
called Anish Kapoor to use it,

00:00:54.720 --> 00:00:56.769
and that annoyed a lot of artists,

00:00:56.769 --> 00:00:58.480
and I got really annoyed as well.

00:00:58.480 --> 00:01:00.690
So I put my Pinkest Pink out.

00:01:00.690 --> 00:01:02.879
On the web site I put a disclaimer

00:01:02.880 --> 00:01:05.780
that you weren't Anish Kapoor,
you weren't associated with Anish Kapoor.

00:01:05.780 --> 00:01:08.320
A lot of people thought that was quite funny

00:01:08.329 --> 00:01:10.770
and a lot of people bought the pink

00:01:10.770 --> 00:01:14.670
unfortunately including Anish Kapoor,
who managed to get his hands on it

00:01:14.670 --> 00:01:16.290
and then he dipped his finger in it

00:01:16.290 --> 00:01:19.250
and put it on Instagram
that he'd got the pink.

00:01:19.250 --> 00:01:22.310
It was sort of great and sort of really frustrating
when he did that,

00:01:22.310 --> 00:01:25.240
because all of a sudden we were getting
thousands and thousands of orders

00:01:25.240 --> 00:01:29.270
we ended up staying up for about four weeks
making pink paint

00:01:29.270 --> 00:01:30.399
and nothing else could happen

00:01:30.399 --> 00:01:32.260
and it just took over our whole life.

00:01:32.260 --> 00:01:36.390
There are all sorts of complicated and clever
ways that computers can store colour,

00:01:36.390 --> 00:01:38.560
but by the time this video reaches your screen

00:01:38.560 --> 00:01:42.040
it's basically just a series of numbers that
say how bright

00:01:42.040 --> 00:01:44.829
to make each of the red, green and blue pixels,

00:01:44.829 --> 00:01:47.880
the tiny lights that make up your screen.

00:01:47.880 --> 00:01:50.840
And unless you've got
a really fancy, expensive screen,

00:01:50.840 --> 00:01:55.909
those numbers run from 0 to 255 --
which is eight 1s in binary.

00:01:55.909 --> 00:02:01.039
0 means turn the pixel off,
255 means as bright as it can go.

00:02:01.039 --> 00:02:05.950
That means your screen can show
somewhere around 16 million colours.

00:02:05.950 --> 00:02:07.229
But that's not every colour.

00:02:07.229 --> 00:02:08.869
And it's not this one.

00:02:08.869 --> 00:02:12.340
Because if I pointed this camera at the sun,
you wouldn't be blinded.

00:02:12.340 --> 00:02:17.430
The numbers can't go above 255, I can't make
your screen go brighter than 100%.

00:02:17.430 --> 00:02:22.800
And if I point it at something extremely dark,
the number can't go below 0.

00:02:22.800 --> 00:02:26.560
I can't make your screen any darker
than if it was turned off.

00:02:26.560 --> 00:02:29.430
In the same way, there are colours that are
so intense

00:02:29.430 --> 00:02:33.501
your screen can't display them, the pixels
cannot go red, green or blue enough.

00:02:33.501 --> 00:02:35.459
And this is one of them.

00:02:35.459 --> 00:02:38.180
There are massive libraries of pigments,

00:02:38.180 --> 00:02:42.800
organic ones, inorganic ones, synthetic ones,
created ones, natural ones,

00:02:42.800 --> 00:02:45.379
and they've been digging them up
since day dot.

00:02:45.379 --> 00:02:47.989
'Mummy brown' was made out of ground-up mummies.

00:02:47.989 --> 00:02:52.160
So there's a huge database of pigments that
you can order.

00:02:52.160 --> 00:02:53.450
And you can play with them.

00:02:53.450 --> 00:02:55.800
I've made mixes of these pigments and binders,

00:02:55.800 --> 00:03:00.239
created something, tweaked it, and gone back
and got these relationships right.

00:03:00.240 --> 00:03:03.100
So it's quite a complicated little potion,
actually.

00:03:03.100 --> 00:03:05.900
In a few years' time,
technology will move on.

00:03:05.909 --> 00:03:09.879
It's already possible to buy expensive equipment
with a wider colour gamut,

00:03:09.879 --> 00:03:12.310
and that might be able to display this pink.

00:03:12.310 --> 00:03:16.940
But in the meantime, I can try and simulate
what being here is like.

00:03:16.940 --> 00:03:21.269
In this shot, this one here, I've digitally
adjusted the saturation of this pink

00:03:21.269 --> 00:03:23.380
so it's not quite as intense.

00:03:23.380 --> 00:03:25.300
Now if I turn it back up...

00:03:26.600 --> 00:03:29.620
Imagine that change, but more so.

00:03:29.620 --> 00:03:31.129
That's how pink this pink is.

00:03:31.129 --> 00:03:33.420
It is ridiculously pink.

00:03:34.340 --> 00:03:36.320
Thank you to Stuart Semple and all the team
at his workshop:

00:03:36.320 --> 00:03:40.940
pull down the description for more about them,
their pigments, and this pink.

00:03:41.880 --> 00:03:43.120
This is gorgeous!

00:03:43.129 --> 00:03:46.519
I cannot get over how luminous this is.

00:03:46.519 --> 00:03:49.739
I started out, like, "well, this isn't doing
anything, this is just powder"

00:03:49.739 --> 00:03:51.629
and then it just goes!

